Bonus Task: In-Memory Cache

Goal:
Improve application performance by adding a simple in-memory caching mechanism for frequently accessed data. In this project, the getAllBooks() method (list of all books) is cached.

Implementation:

A new package com.library.cache was created.

The Cache interface was defined with the following methods:

void put(String key, Object value) – stores an object in the cache with the given key.

Optional<Object> get(String key) – retrieves an object from the cache.

void remove(String key) – removes an entry by key.

void clear() – clears the entire cache.

The InMemoryCache implementation uses a thread-safe ConcurrentHashMap. The class is implemented as a Singleton (private constructor, static getInstance() method), ensuring a single cache instance throughout the application.

In the BookService class, the following were added:

A field cache = InMemoryCache.getInstance().

A constant ALL_BOOKS_KEY = "allBooks".

The getAllBooks() method now first checks if data exists in the cache. If the key is present, the cached value is returned (with a log message: "Returning cached list of books"). If not, the data is loaded from the database, stored in the cache, and then returned.

In the createBook, updateBook, and deleteBook methods, after a successful database operation, cache.remove(ALL_BOOKS_KEY) is called, which invalidates the cache (log: "Cache invalidated after ...").

Benefits:

Reduces database load on repeated requests for the book list.

Transparent invalidation on data changes – ensures data remains up-to-date.

Simple and lightweight – the cache is stored in memory and requires no external systems.

Complies with SOLID principles: the interface separates abstraction from implementation, the Singleton is implemented correctly, and the cache does not mix with business logic.

Thread-safe due to the use of ConcurrentHashMap.
